General agents, snapshot-isolated evals & environments — the formal plan #agents #evals #environments #design #plan

The settled (and still-open) design for three intertwined things that turned out to be
**one mechanism**: a permission tier for **general agents**, **snapshot-isolated evals**, and
**dev/staging/production environments**. Captured so no detail is lost as we build. This is
**our cloud product** (dogfood workbooks via the DeployKit), NOT the open-standard nexus image —
see [[the-line]]. Companion to [[design]] (the agent service). Tracking: bd `wb-m52l` (foundation),
`wb-bhyk` (environments), `wb-hqu5` (eval harness).

## The unifying insight

An **eval**, a per-user **dev tree**, and a **safe agent run** are the SAME primitive: a
**snapshot-isolated overlay** of the workspace tree that you then either **discard** (restore the
snapshot) or **promote** (integrate forward). Build that primitive once and all three fall out.
We are NOT building three separate apps or three subsystems — one nexus, branch/overlays of one
tree, jj for the merges, a human gate before anything reaches production.

## 1 — The agent permission model (four independent axes)

An agent block declares capability along four ORTHOGONAL axes. Three exist today; the fourth
(**scope**) is the new tier this plan adds.

- **`capabilities`** — toolkit admission: `:all` (workhorse) · `:none` (autopoet) · `[ids]`.
  (Built — `Nexus.Agent.capabilities/admits?/admitted`.)
- **`grant`** — host capabilities the run gets: `fs`, `exec`, `browse`, `commands`. (Built.)
- **`ceiling`** — the subtree edit boundary the autopoet may autonomously edit within. (Built —
  the structural triad grant/ceiling/management.)
- **`scope`** — NEW. WHICH workspaces the agent operates over:
  - **workspace-scoped** (default) — the run is a jj worktree of ONE workspace; confined to `/work`.
  - **general** — operates over the WHOLE workspace tree (root of all workspaces, **NOT** the host
    filesystem root). Can create / edit / delete / manage **any** workspace. **workhorse** and
    **autopoet** are general; **must be declared explicitly** (a deliberate, server-enforced grant).
    Adding another general agent is allowed but always explicit. See [[general-vs-workspace-agents]].

A "**general** workspace" = the root level across all workspaces (what a general run sees as `/work`).

## 2 — jj snapshot / restore (BUILT — wb-m52l phase 1)

`Nexus.JJ.snapshot(dir)` captures the current op-log head (a short hex op-id);
`Nexus.JJ.restore(dir, op_id)` runs `jj op restore <op_id>` + re-export, reverting the working
copy + bookmarks to that op and discarding everything since (op-id re-validated). Verified with
jj 0.39. This is the per-repo isolation primitive. (`nexus/lib/jj.ex`; test `git_jj_test.exs`.)

**Open:** a workspace is its OWN repo, so a TREE-level snapshot (for a general run that creates a
NEW workspace) must capture {set of workspaces, per-workspace op-id}. Restore = delete workspaces
created since + `restore/2` each pre-existing one. Build as `Nexus.Eval`/tree-snapshot on top of
`snapshot/2`. (Phase 3 below.)

## 3 — General-agent run model (DECIDED: tree-root + auto-register) — wb-m52l phase 2

- A **general** run mounts `/work` = the **workspace-tree root** (all workspaces as subdirs), not a
  single worktree, not the host fs root.
- The agent makes a new top-level dir + files → on **finalize**, the runtime **registers any new
  top-level dir as a real workspace** (CP record + bare repo at `/git/<id>.git` + working tree +
  mount — reuse `Nexus.Git.provision_remote` / the platform create path). Removed top-level dirs
  **deprovision** (and clean up the repos — fixes the known cascade-delete gap at `platform.ex:315`).
- **Enforcement:** only `scope: general` agents get tree-root `/work`; a workspace-scoped agent can
  never reach it. Gate server-side in the run path (`Nexus.Agent.setup_vfs`).
- Likely also expose an explicit lifecycle action (a `workspace` kit verb) later for auditability;
  start with auto-register (the decided mechanism).

## 4 — Snapshot-isolated evals — wb-hqu5

An eval run = snapshot the tree → run the (general) agent → score → **restore** the snapshot.
Re-runnable from an identical baseline; **zero production pollution**. "Dev mode" for an agent is the
same thing without the auto-restore (you keep the overlay to review/promote). The marketing-workspace
eval ("workhorse, create a `marketing` workspace + skeleton") runs here.

## 5 — Environments: dev / staging / production (NOT three apps) — wb-bhyk

Environments are **refs/overlays over the ONE workspace tree on the ONE nexus** (the push-to-deploy
overlay model already in `Nexus.Server` + jj). No extra deployments. See [[one-nexus-per-org]].

- **dev** — per-user, MANY, cheap. Your own overlay/branch off prod (a snapshot-isolated workspace
  tree). The nexus serves it **to you**. You maintain your own dev tree.
- **staging** — exactly ONE, shared (multi-user). A PR target ("we agree this is next"). Managing PRs
  from many dev trees → one staging.
- **production** — the ONE served default (what everyone gets).
- **Promotion** = PR / `Nexus.JJ.integrate` dev → staging → prod (jj CRDT merges).
- **Switching** = a selector in user settings; the nexus serves you whichever ref. Using an agent MAY
  auto-switch your view to your dev overlay so you see changes live.

### Agents in the environment model (the "don't go nuclear" guarantee)

- General agents (workhorse/autopoet) **never edit production directly** — they work on a **dev
  overlay**, snapshot-isolated. Worst case: restore the snapshot, as if nothing happened.
- **workhorse (interactive)** — running it auto-switches your Studio view to your dev overlay; you
  watch changes land live; a **HITL "promote"** step opens the PR to staging/prod.
- **autopoet (headless)** — works on a dev overlay, opens a **PR with a diff**; you review in
  chat/Studio and promote. Never auto-touches prod.

## 5b — Live transport: SSE today, WebSockets next (wb-a4sw)

The runtime ALREADY anticipates this — `Nexus.Rcp` advertises `transports: %{http: true, ws: false}`,
a wired-in slot to flip on. So WebSockets are the planned next transport on the existing `Nexus.Live`
channel, not a rewrite.

**STATUS:** server foundation ✅ BUILT (commit 4660c41f) — `Nexus.Ws` duplex handler over `Nexus.Live`,
`GET /ws` (auth + Bandit upgrade), server-injected identity (also fixed the `/live` SSE tenant-from-query
hole), RCP `transports.ws=true`, `agent_run` threads model/isolate/capabilities. Browser/Studio can
consume now. Remaining: Zig CLI WS client (`work agent run` over /ws), live integration test, mid-run
steer/cancel, Studio Runs → /ws.

- **Where WS wins (build it):** the CLIENT↔nexus live channel. SSE already streams run output one-way;
  WS adds the BIDIRECTIONAL half — mid-run **steering/interrupt**, **HITL approve-in-stream**, presence,
  multi-stage progress, and the live view of agents/sub-agents working. This subsumes wb-scmk (stream
  runs over the WS channel; CLI + Studio both consume it). The "correct synchronous run" becomes a live
  WS session that blocks until done and can be steered.
- **Where WS is NOT the answer:** (a) agent↔agent WITHIN one nexus = BEAM processes + `Nexus.Events`
  (faster than a socket, already there); WS is the NETWORK-boundary transport (client↔nexus, nexus↔nexus).
  (b) nexus↔MODEL — keep HTTP completions. **DECISION (wb-a9qa):** don't adopt OpenAI's WebSocket
  Realtime API for the text brain — it's voice-oriented + OpenAI-only, and CF-AI-Gateway compat is
  questionable; our agents run GLM via the gateway (HTTP). Revisit Realtime only for VOICE agents.
- **Sub-agents on a shared workspace (wb-7dbx):** the same jj substrate — each sub-agent its own
  worktree (`workspace_add`), snapshot/tag baselines, merge via `integrate` (CRDT) — with the human
  watching live over the WS channel. Intra-workspace concurrency = eval-isolation primitives reused.

## 6 — Open questions (do NOT lose these)

- Tree-level snapshot/restore shape (per-workspace op-ids + workspace-set diff) — §2 open.
- Does using an agent **auto-switch** the user's visual environment to their dev overlay, or is it
  headless with review-in-chat / HITL? (Leaning: workhorse auto-switch + watch; autopoet headless PR.)
- Where the env selector lives (user settings) + how a session pins its env.
- staging: is it a single shared branch with PR queue, or per-feature? (Leaning: one staging, PR queue.)
- Is staging == production sometimes (small orgs)? (Possibly collapse to dev+prod.)
- Deprovision/cleanup semantics on workspace delete (cascade repos + CP + mount).
- How `work` CLI selects env (`--env dev|staging|prod`) for headless agent/eval runs.

## 7 — Phased build + status

1. **jj snapshot/restore** — ✅ DONE (wb-m52l P1).
2. **General-agent scope tier** — ✅ BUILT (wb-0fbq): `scope general` (workhorse/autopilot); a general
   run mounts `/work` = isolated staging COPY of the whole workspace tree (never `.nexus`); on success
   new top-level dirs auto-register as workspaces (`Nexus.Workspaces.sync_back`); `:general` forced from
   the declared scope (no caller escalation). `Nexus.Workspaces` (list/provision/deprovision/stage/
   sync_back/snapshot/restore) unit-tested. Pending: live verification on the deployed nexus.
3. **Snapshot-isolated evals** — ✅ VERIFIED LIVE (wb-hsrj). `--isolate` = the general run works ONLY in
   its staging copy, never syncs back → production touched zero times (no provision, no remount). The
   marketing eval ran on wb-dogfood in 44s synchronously: workhorse created marketing/index.work +
   campaigns/content/assets, production stayed cloud/lander/docs (no leak), run logged. Incremental mount
   (`Nexus.Server.mount_one`) replaces the recompile-all remount for the persist path; `DELETE
   /workspaces` now cascades (dir+repo+CP) via `Nexus.Workspaces.deprovision`.
   - LEARNED: a blocking POST can't survive a long run (proxy timeout) — short evals fit, long runs need
     STREAMING (wb-scmk): server already streams via `/live/agent_run` SSE (Studio uses it); the CLI must
     consume it. That's the "correct synchronous run." Also the persist path (visible workspace) needs it
     for long runs even with incremental mount.
4. **Environments** — env selector + serve-which-ref + PR/promote UX (wb-bhyk), built on 1–3. TODO.
5. **Agent UX** — workhorse auto-switch-to-dev + HITL promote; autopoet headless PR. TODO.

## 8 — Eval findings already banked (the loop is working)

- ✅ **Kit substrate** (wb-gx3y) — deployed agents had no wasmtime + no kit wasm; FIXED + verified
  live (workhorse runs real coreutils on GLM 5.2 via the CF AI Gateway).
- ✅ **scrape/render crash** (wb-dwsi) — `Extract.tidy/1` got an iolist title → `String.replace/4`
  crash; FIXED + regression test.
- ✅ **Sandbox is tight** (security verified) — a run is confined to a single WASI pre-opened `/work`;
  can't escape, read host env/secrets, or write outside its workspace.
- 📋 bash isn't POSIX (no `>`/`;`/`2>&1`, only pipes + `tee`); the `web` kit summary misleads the
  agent into `web fetch` (command is `fetch`); `do_run` swallows run stacktraces (log `__STACKTRACE__`).

